# XSH sigsuspend()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.

assertion  XSH.sigsuspend.001
anchor     functions/sigsuspend.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       sigsuspend() with an empty mask, while SIGUSR1 is blocked and
           pending, runs the handler, then returns -1 with errno EINTR,
           and the mask is again the one before the call (SIGUSR1
           blocked).
test       t/001-handler-restores.c

assertion  XSH.sigsuspend.002
anchor     functions/sigsuspend.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       With nothing pending, sigsuspend() does not return until a caught
           signal sent 300 ms later is delivered.
test       t/002-waits.c

assertion  XSH.sigsuspend.003
anchor     functions/sigsuspend.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       While sigsuspend() waits with a mask that blocks SIGUSR2, a
           SIGUSR2 sent first stays pending and a later SIGUSR1 ends the
           wait; SIGUSR2 is delivered only after the old mask (nothing
           blocked) is back.
test       t/003-mask-during.c

assertion  XSH.sigsuspend.004
anchor     functions/sigsuspend.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       A thread in sigsuspend() that gets a signal whose action is to
           terminate (SIGTERM at SIG_DFL) does not return: the process
           ends by that signal.
nomutant   termination is done by the kernel
test       t/004-terminates.c

assertion  XSH.sigsuspend.005
anchor     functions/sigsuspend.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       sigsuspend() with every signal in its mask still lets SIGKILL
           through: the waiting process is killed.
nomutant   the kernel keeps SIGKILL out of the mask
test       t/005-kill-unblockable.c
